# sar — real-world examples

`sar` (from the sysstat package) collects and displays system activity. Related
tools: `sadc` (data collector backend), `sa1`/`sa2` (cron collectors/summaries),
`sadf` (export to CSV/XML), plus `iostat`, `mpstat`, `pidstat`, `nfsiostat`,
`cifsiostat`. Reference: https://www.thegeekstuff.com/2011/03/sar-examples/

## Setup on the homelab (OpenSUSE Tumbleweed)

```sh
sudo zypper install sysstat
sudo systemctl enable --now sysstat            # starts periodic collection
# data lands in /var/log/sa/saNN (NN = day of month)
```

## CPU usage (-u)

```sh
sar -u                       # CPU usage collected so far today
sar -u 1 3                   # real-time: every 1s, 3 samples
sar -u ALL                   # same, with extra fields
sar -u -f /var/log/sa/sa10   # replay the 10th of the month
```

## Per-core CPU (-P)

```sh
sar -P ALL                   # all cores, today
sar -P ALL 1 3               # all cores, every 1s, 3 samples
sar -P 1                     # core 1 only
sar -P 1 1 3                 # core 1, real-time
```

## Memory, swap, paging

```sh
sar -r    # memory free/used
sar -S    # swap space stats
sar -R    # memory pages freed/used/cached per second
sar -H    # hugepages used/available (KB)
sar -B    # paging statistics (KB paged in/out per second)
sar -W    # page swap in/out per second
```

## Network (-n)

```sh
sar -n DEV      # per-interface throughput (eth0, eth1, ...)
sar -n EDEV     # interface error stats
sar -n TCP      # TCPv4 traffic
sar -n SOCK     # sockets in use (IPv4)
sar -n ALL      # everything (long output)
# Also: NFS, NFSD, IP, EIP, ICMP, EICMP, UDP, and *6 variants for IPv6
```

## Homelab examples

```sh
# Watch disk/CPU while a Plex transcode runs on mediaserver
sar -u 2 30

# Did the box swap overnight? Replay yesterday's file (say the 10th)
sar -S -f /var/log/sa/sa10

# Network saturation during a big rsync
sar -n DEV 1 20

# Export a day's CPU data to CSV for a spreadsheet
sadf -d /var/log/sa/sa10 -- -u > cpu10.csv
```

## Companion tools

```sh
iostat -xz 2      # extended per-device I/O stats
mpstat -P ALL 2   # per-core CPU
pidstat 2         # per-process CPU/memory over time
```
